      "problem": "The graph of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ with $a<0$ has discriminant $D<0.$ Which of the following could be the graph of this function?",
      "worked_solution": "To find the possible graph of the parabola, we need to determine two things:\n\n- The direction in which the parabola opens.\n\n- The number of distinct real roots of the quadratic function.\n\nSince $a<0,$ the parabola opens downward. Moreover, since the discriminant $D<0,$ the quadratic function has no real roots. So, it should not intersect the $x$-axis.\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the only graph that satisfies both these conditions is the following:\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266874/assets/eabf139b9d3b6ddbf09553cc4e24eb6b1430f558e9120d777c9dfa1815b4cb53.png)",
